## Why This Is Asked

Tests the ability to design end-to-end checks for multi-tenant isolation, safe canary rollouts, per-tenant quotas, and rollback safety, all under realistic load patterns and with observability built in.

## Key Concepts

- Multi-tenant data isolation and namespaces
- Canary feature flag rollout and traffic shaping
- Per-tenant quotas and burst load handling
- Data leakage prevention across tenants
- Rollback safety and path reversions
- Observability: traces, metrics, logs, alerting

## Code Example

```javascript
// Example: toggle canary flag for a tenant and verify routing change
async function testCanaryTenant(tenantId){
  await api.setFeatureFlag({tenantId, flag: 'NEW_SEARCH', value: true, rollout: 0.2});
  const traces = await tracing.collect(tenantId);
  const path = traces.find(t => t.name === 'searchBackend');
  assert(path.usedNewBackend === true);
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you validate rollback safety under observed latency spikes?
- What metrics and alerting thresholds would you define for this rollout?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests for autosave/drafts cover offline-first UX, idempotent writes, and cross-device conflicts, which are common pain points in real apps.

## Key Concepts
- Offline-first data flows and write queuing
- Idempotent REST writes and ordering guarantees
- Test data isolation via seeds and reset hooks

## Code Example
```javascript
// Playwright skeleton (pseudo)
test('autosave offline queue and merge', async ({ page, request }) => {
  await page.goto('/editor');
  await page.fill('#note', 'Initial');
  await page.waitForTimeout(31000); // wait for autosave
  // simulate offline
  await page.route('POST', '/api/drafts', route => route.abort());
  await page.fill('#note', 'Offline edit');
  await page.setOfflineMode(true);
  await page.fill('#note', 'More offline edits');
  await page.setOfflineMode(false);
  // verify upload order and no duplicates
  // reload or inspect draft store
});
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you adapt this for WebSocket-based drafting?
- How would you test long offline periods and partial merges?

## Why This Question Matters

This question assesses your understanding of end-to-end data isolation, cross-region replication, retention governance, and PII redaction across a data lake, transformed layers, and BI dashboards—essential skills for building enterprise-compliant multi-tenant systems.

## Key Concepts to Demonstrate

- Multi-tenant data isolation across raw and transformed layers
- Per-tenant retention and purge workflows with full auditability
- Cross-region replication latency and consistency guarantees
- PII redaction verification across caches and dashboards
- Seed data design and deterministic testing signals

## Code Example

```javascript
// Seed tenants for deterministic E2E tests
const tenants = [
  { id: 't1', name: 'Acme Corp', region: 'us-east-1' },
  { id: 't2', name: 'Globex', region: 'eu-west-1' }
];

// Simulate event streams from mobile and web
async function seedEventStream(tenant, source) {
  const events = generateEvents(tenant.id, source, 1000);
  await publishToIngestTopic(tenant.region, events);
}

// Verify isolation: ensure t1 cannot access t2 data
async function verifyIsolation() {
  const t1Raw = await queryRawLake('t1');
  const t2Raw = await queryRawLake('t2');
  expect(t1Raw).not.toContainTenantData('t2');
  expect(t2Raw).not.toContainTenantData('t1');
}

// Verify PII redaction in transformed views
async function verifyRedaction() {
  const transformed = await queryTransformedView('t1');
  expect(transformed).toHaveRedacted('email');
  expect(transformed).toHaveRedacted('phone');
}
```

## Verification Matrix

| Layer | Isolation | Redaction | Retention |
|-------|-----------|-----------|------------|
| Raw Lake | ✓ | N/A | ✓ |
| Transformed | ✓ | ✓ | ✓ |
| Cache | ✓ | ✓ | N/A |
| BI Dashboard | ✓ | ✓ | N/A |

## Essential Metrics

- **Isolation violations**: Zero cross-tenant data leaks
- **Redaction coverage**: 100% of defined PII fields
- **Replication lag**: < 5s for sync, < 60s for async regions
- **Retention accuracy**: Auto-purge at tenant-specified TTL
- **Query latency**: P95 < 500ms for dashboard queries

## Why This Is Asked
Tests for offline-first applications with service workers and cross-tab interactions are common in modern web development. This question evaluates practical E2E thinking around offline caching, network replay, idempotent API design, and conflict resolution at a beginner level.

## Key Concepts
- Offline-first UX and service workers
- Local storage and IndexedDB persistence
- Idempotent server updates with PUT
- Cross-tab concurrency and conflict handling
- Deterministic data seeds for repeatable tests

## Code Example
```javascript
// Playwright E2E test for offline-first note app
import { test, expect } from '@playwright/test';

test.describe('Offline-first note app', () => {
  test('should create note offline, sync when online, and handle idempotency', async ({ page }) => {
    // Set up deterministic seed for reproducible data
    const noteId = 'test-note-001';
    const noteContent = 'Test note content';

    // Simulate offline by intercepting network requests
    await page.route('**/api/**', (route) => route.abort('failed'));

    // Create note while offline
    await page.goto('/app');
    await page.fill('[data-testid="note-editor"]', noteContent);
    await page.click('[data-testid="save-button"]');

    // Verify note is cached locally
    const localNote = await page.evaluate(() => localStorage.getItem('draft'));
    expect(localNote).toContain(noteContent);

    // Restore network and mock PUT endpoint
    await page.unroute('**/api/**');
    await page.route('**/api/notes/**', (route) => {
      route.fulfill({ status: 200, body: JSON.stringify({ revision: 'rev-123' }) });
    });

    // Trigger sync
    await page.click('[data-testid="sync-button"]');
    await expect(page.locator('[data-testid="sync-status"]')).toHaveText('Synced');

    // Verify idempotent behavior - re-submit should not create duplicate
    const initialRequestCount = 1;
    await page.click('[data-testid="sync-button"]');
    // Assert no duplicate entries on server
  });
});
```

## Why This Is Asked
Tests at mobile viewport ensure responsive UI behavior; verifying data seeding and isolation prevents flaky cross-test interactions; skeleton loaders verification catches performance UX issues during data fetch.

## Key Concepts
- Per-run data seeding and isolation
- Mobile-first E2E flow: search, filter, add-to-cart, checkout
- Skeleton/loading state verification
- API interception/mocking for payments

