## Why This Is Asked
Tests data isolation, asynchronous ETL, eventual consistency, and idempotency in a real-world multi-tenant dashboard.

## Key Concepts
- Tenant isolation
- Mocking external data sources
- Idempotent operations
- Eventual consistency and latency
- Regional replication and failover

## Code Example
```javascript
// Playwright-like sketch
test('dataset ETL end-to-end with tenant isolation', async ({page}) => {
  const tenant = await createTenant();
  const dsId = `ds-${Date.now()}`;
  await loginAsTenantUser(tenant);
  await page.goto('/datasets');
  await page.fill('#name', dsId);
  await page.click('#create');
  // mock external sources and trigger ETL via API
  await mockExternalDataForTenant(tenant, dsId);
  await triggerETL(dsId);
  await waitForDashboardData(tenant, dsId);
  // idempotency check
  await page.click('#create', {force:true});
  await waitForDashboardData(tenant, dsId);
});
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you validate data correctness if ETL shows partial results due to a region outage?
- What metrics would you collect to monitor test determinism across runs?

## Why This Is Asked

Tests for multi-tenant isolation are foundational for SaaS apps. The goal is to confirm UI and API boundaries prevent cross-tenant data leakage, a common subtle bug.

## Key Concepts

- Multi-tenancy data isolation
- Role-based access control (RBAC)
- Deterministic fixtures for repeatable runs
- UI vs API boundary testing

## Code Example

```javascript
// Cypress skeleton for tenant isolation
describe('Tenant isolation', () => {
  beforeEach(() => {
    seedTenants(['A','B']);
  });
  it('creates user in A and enforces isolation', () => {
    cy.visit('/login');
    cy.login('adminA','A');
    cy.visit('/admin/users');
    cy.get('[data-cy=create-user]').click();
    cy.get('[data-cy=username]').type('newuser');
    cy.get('[data-cy=save]').click();
    cy.contains('newuser').should('exist');
    cy.request({method:'GET', url:'/api/users?tenant=A'}).then(resp => {
      expect(resp.body.some(u => u.username==='newuser')).to.be.true;
    });
    cy.request({method:'GET', url:'/api/users?tenant=B'}).then(resp => {
      expect(resp.body.some(u => u.username==='newuser')).to.be.false;
    });
  });
});
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you scale this for 100 tenants while keeping tests fast?
- How would you handle flaky network simulations and ensure determinism across runs?

## Why This Is Asked

Tests a complex, cross-origin integration scenario with iframe messaging, localStorage persistence, and multi-tenant isolation—common in partner ecosystems like Salesforce, Cloudflare, and Coinbase. It probes reliability under network blips and ensures data integrity across domains.

## Key Concepts

- Cross-origin messaging via postMessage between host and iframe
- Frame targeting with frameLocator/selectors
- Draft persistence in localStorage and autosave recovery
- Network fault injection and retry semantics
- Tenant data isolation across partner domains

## Code Example

```javascript
// Playwright sketch (conceptual only)
const host = page.locator('#host-app');
const frame = host.frameLocator('iframe#widget-form');
await frame.locator('input[name="name"]').fill('Acme Corp');
// simulate cross-origin event
await page.evaluate(() => {
  window.postMessage({type: 'INIT', tenant: 'tenantA'}, '*');
});
// flaky submit
await page.route('https://api.partner.com/submit', route => {
  route.fulfill({ status: 500, body: '{}' });
});
await frame.locator('button[type="submit"]').click();
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you test race conditions between postMessage handlers across both domains?
- How would you validate that autosaved drafts don’t leak between tenants when the host cache is cleared?

## Why This Is Asked

This question probes end-to-end coverage for real-time, multi-tenant architectures with RBAC, data isolation, and performance under load, mirroring production concerns in large-scale analytics platforms.

## Key Concepts

- Real-time WebSocket testing at scale (1k streams)
- Strict per-tenant data isolation to prevent leakage
- Dynamic RBAC changes mid-session and their immediate effects
- Deterministic data seeds for repeatable tests
- Network throttling and latency percentile validation

## Code Example

```javascript
// Pseudo-test outline (outline only)
async function testTenantDashboardScale() {
  await provisionTenant({ id: 'tenantA' });
  const wsPool = new WebSocketPool('/stream/tenantA', 1000);
  await wsPool.connectAll();
  // emit synthetic events, verify aggregates stay tenant-scoped
  await verifyIsolation('tenantA');
  await simulateRBACChangeMidSession('tenantA', 'viewer', 'editor');
  await verifyAccess('tenantA', 'editor');
  await throttleNetwork(1000);
  await verifyLatencyPercentile('tenantA', 95);
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you validate no cross-tenant data leakage under peak load?  
- How would you parameterize scales (1k vs 5k) and ensure test determinism across runs?

## Why This Is Asked
E2E with ML serving is common in production; tests must cover routing, feature delivery, latency, drift, and rollback.

## Key Concepts
- Canary vs Shadow routing
- Feature store integration and data drift monitoring
- Multi-region consistency and rollback triggers
- Observability and SLO validation

## Code Example
```javascript
// Pseudo-test harness for ML-serving end-to-end
async function runETest(seed) {
  await seedFeatureStore(seed);
  const a = await requestModel('A', seed);
  const b = await requestModel('B', seed);
  assertClose(a.pred, b.pred, tol=0.01);
  assertLatency(b.latency, 400);
  // simulate drift and verify rollback path
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you implement drift thresholds and alerting?  
- How would you test rollback under partial failure (region loss)?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests for offline-first PWAs are a common beginner challenge, ensuring service workers and syncing work under real user conditions.

## Key Concepts
- Offline-first UX and service worker caching
- E2E data seeding for deterministic tests
- Network control via intercepts (cy.intercept / route)
- Idempotent sync and duplicate prevention

## Code Example
```javascript
// Cypress example sketch
cy.request('POST', '/api/test/setup', {user:'test', products:[101,102]});
cy.visit('/');
cy.intercept('/api/cart', { statusCode: 503 }).as('cartOffline');
cy.get('#add-101').click();
cy.get('#add-102').click();
cy.intercept('/api/cart', (req) => req.continue()).as('cartOnline');
cy.wait('@cartOffline');
cy.get('#checkout').click();
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you adapt this for a multi-user scenario with concurrent offline sessions?
- What metrics would you collect to ensure the offline sync is fast enough for a good UX?
